Dear Dr. Pereira,
#v(0em)

On behalf of my coauthors, I am excited to submit an article entitled _A protocol for biodiversity-informed wildlife disease surveillance_ to be considered for publication as a research article in _PLOS Ecosystems_.

In this manuscript, we present a protocol for incorporating biodiversity data into the design of biosurveillance programs by utilizing species distribution models for hosts of zoonotic pathogens, and illustrate this protocol's flexibility to adapt to differing levels of data availability with two case studies. 

// todo: talk about integrating biodiv data with disease data 
It is well understood that there is a complex, context-dependent relationship between biodiversity and the prevalence of zoonotic disease, which is further complicated by the impacts of anthropogenic change. This work provides practical guidance toward improve our understanding of the relationship between biodiversity and pathogens, which is necessary to better assess spillover risk and guide public health action.

We believe this work is highly relevant to readers of _PLOS Ecosystems_ interested in disease ecology,  human-wildlife interactions, and biodiversity monitoring and biosurveillance from a One Health perspective.

We confirm this research is our original work and has not been published elsewhere, and that this work has been read and approved for submission to _PLOS Ecosystems_ by all coauthors. We have no conflicts of interest to disclose. 

Please address all correspondence to _michael.catchen\@umontreal.ca_. 

Thank you for taking the time to consider this manuscript.

#v(0.5em)
Sincerely,\
Michael D. Catchen, Ph.D.